\subsection{Decoupling}\label{ssec:decoupling}

The per-example findings above raise a natural question: when aggregate metrics converge, does this reflect agreement on the same examples? Table~\ref{tab:convergence} provides context. Only LAMBADA shows monotonic convergence ($\rho = -1.0$, Bonferroni-adjusted $p = .170$; excluding GDN, whose h3-1 uses 16 rather than 32 attention heads: $\rho = -0.7$, $p = .233$; observed at 1.3B, absent at 340M; Appendix~\ref{app:convergence_detail}, Figure~\ref{fig:task_specificity}). This is a suggestive but underpowered secondary observation ($n = 5$; the test has negligible power for $|\rho| < 1.0$). Given the limited statistical power, non-significant correlations on other benchmarks should not be interpreted as absence of association. LAMBADA relies purely on in-context retrieval without external knowledge or multi-step reasoning, making it the task most directly aligned with attention's role in context access---a plausible explanation for why convergence is strongest here.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_task_specificity.pdf}
\caption{Spearman $\rho$ between attention ratio and cross-architecture $\sigma$ for each benchmark at 1.3B scale. LAMBADA ($\rho = -1.0$; BCa 95\% CI $[-1.0,\,-0.7]$, heuristic) is the only benchmark with a negative correlation whose confidence interval excludes zero. Benchmarks are grouped by task category. Bootstrap CI is heuristic given $n = 3$ architectures.}
\label{fig:task_specificity}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/jaccard_heatmap_5arch.pdf}
\caption{Pairwise Jaccard heatmap across five architectures (340M, h3-1), where helped (hurt) examples are those correct only with (without) attention. Upper triangle: $J_{\text{helped}}$; lower triangle: $J_{\text{hurt}}$. All values $\leq 0.14$, all permutation tests $p \leq .002$. HellaSwag and PIQA results in Appendix~\ref{app:extended_arch}.}
\label{fig:jaccard_heatmap_5arch}
\end{figure*}

Even on LAMBADA, the answer is no. Aggregate convergence and per-example divergence operate on different timescales as attention ratio increases (Figure~\ref{fig:decoupling}).

Cross-architecture $\sigma$ decreases from 0.024 (pure) to 0.005 (h3-1), while pairwise Jaccard saturates at the first attention level ($\bar{J} \approx 0.095$) with no further increase---indicating that each architecture's per-example response to attention is largely idiosyncratic. The observed $\bar{J}$ reaches only 6--7\% of the achievable range above chance ($J_{\max} \approx 0.78$--$0.92$), ruling out ceiling constraints (\S\ref{ssec:per_example}).

This saturation generalizes beyond LAMBADA (Figure~\ref{fig:jaccard_saturation}): on all four main benchmarks, mean pairwise Jaccard shows no significant trend with attention ratio (all $\rho \leq 0.80$, $p \geq 0.333$), with values uniformly between 0.08 and 0.11.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig4_jaccard_saturation.pdf}
\caption{Cross-benchmark Jaccard saturation. Mean pairwise $\bar{J}$ remains flat across attention ratios on all four benchmarks (all $\rho \leq 0.80$, $p \geq .333$), confirming that per-example divergence saturates immediately regardless of task type.}
\label{fig:jaccard_saturation}
\end{figure}

Bootstrap 95\% CIs confirm non-overlapping $\sigma$ between pure and h3-1 but completely overlapping Jaccard across all hybrid ratios (\S\ref{ssec:statistical_methods}). Figure~\ref{fig:jaccard_hierarchy} shows a three-tier Jaccard hierarchy: independence baseline (${\sim}0.05$), cross-architecture (${\sim}0.10$, $2\times$ baseline), and within-architecture (${\sim}0.33$, $3\times$ cross-architecture; Appendix~\ref{app:within_arch_stability}). Pythia 70M seed calibration produces a comparable within-seed level ($J = 0.27$--$0.39$; Appendix~\ref{app:seed_calibration}), consistent with architecture-driven divergence, though matched multi-seed experiments would be needed for definitive evidence.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_jaccard_hierarchy.pdf}
\caption{Jaccard hierarchy on LAMBADA (1.3B). Cross-architecture overlap ($\bar{J} \approx 0.09$) is $2\times$ the independence baseline but only one-third of within-architecture overlap ($\bar{J} \approx 0.33$). Pythia 70M within-seed Jaccard (different scale) falls at a comparable level. Error bars show the helped--hurt range.}
\label{fig:jaccard_hierarchy}
\end{figure}

Attention weight analysis (Appendix~\ref{app:attention_patterns}) shows GDN and RetNet produce nearly identical attention distributions (cosine 0.995) yet differ in per-example effects, suggesting these originate in the recurrent layers. The four-benchmark variation (Table~\ref{tab:multiplicity}) confirms independence: LAMBADA ($\alpha = 0.30$) is the only benchmark showing convergence despite having the highest multiplicity, while PIQA ($\alpha = 0.14$) and HellaSwag ($\alpha = 0.15$) show no convergence with the lowest multiplicity.

Cross-scale analysis confirms scale-robustness: pairwise Jaccard is stable ($\bar{J} = 0.08$--$0.10$ at both scales), though $\alpha$ is systematically higher at 340M ($\Delta \leq 8$\,pp). At 340M, aggregate convergence is absent ($\rho = -0.50$, $p = .45$), suggesting that the \emph{pattern} of multiplicity reflects structural differences while absolute magnitude is modulated by capacity (Appendix~\ref{app:scale_per_example}).

\subsection{Robustness}\label{ssec:robustness}

Removing GDN weakens LAMBADA convergence ($\rho = -0.7$, $p = .233$) and reverses MMLU's ($\rho = +0.6$), both driven by GDN's 16-head configuration (vs.\ 32 in others; Appendix~\ref{app:gdn_confound}). The RetNet--HGRN2 pair preserves decoupling ($\sigma \leq 0.008$, $J = 0.086$--$0.108$). The aggregate convergence result is therefore dependent on GDN inclusion; the decoupling result is not. Five-architecture validation (GLA, DeltaNet added) confirms $J \leq 0.14$ across all ten pairs and four benchmarks (all $p < .01$; Figure~\ref{fig:jaccard_heatmap_5arch}; Appendix~\ref{app:extended_arch}). Full sensitivity analyses appear in Appendix~\ref{app:sensitivity}.

\section{Conclusion}

Per-example multiplicity in hybrid recurrent models varies by task ($\alpha = 0.14$--$0.30$), with Jaccard uniformly low ($\leq 0.10$) even as aggregate metrics converge on LAMBADA (dependent on GDN inclusion; \S\ref{ssec:robustness}); five-architecture validation confirms this across four benchmarks (all $J \leq 0.14$, $p < .01$; Appendix~\ref{app:extended_arch}). Pure-model confidence partially predicts affected examples (AUC 0.70--0.74; \S\ref{ssec:per_example}).

For practitioners, three implications follow from the ${\sim}90\%$ non-overlap: (1) $\alpha > 0.2$ warrants per-example audit before architecture substitution, as multiplicity at this level means $>$20\% of examples change outcome; (2) low-Jaccard pairs maximize ensemble complementarity---majority vote fails despite 15--30\% disagreement, but oracle accuracy reveals $+7$--$14$\,pp of untapped gain; (3) rescue-threshold routing is viable when $\alpha$ is high and disruption thresholds differ across architectures, extracting 36\% of oracle complementarity on LAMBADA ($+5.3$\,pp; \S\ref{ssec:per_example}). Deployment-ready routing remains open.

\section{Method}\label{sec:method}

We describe the model configurations, evaluation benchmarks, and analytical framework used to measure how attention affects cross-architecture agreement at both aggregate and per-example levels.

\subsection{Model Zoo}\label{ssec:model_zoo}

We use the pretrained model zoo of \citet{wang2025hybrid}, which provides controlled comparisons across architectures and attention ratios. The zoo contains three linear recurrent architectures: GatedDeltaNet \citep{yang2024gateddeltanet}, RetNet \citep{sun2023retnet}, and HGRN2 \citep{qin2024hgrn2}. These three architectures represent distinct approaches to linear recurrence: GatedDeltaNet uses gated delta rules, RetNet uses multi-scale exponential decay, and HGRN2 uses gated linear recurrence with state expansion. Each architecture is trained at five attention ratios: 0\% (pure recurrent), 4\%, 7.7\%, 14.3\%, and 25\%. We adopt the h$k$-1 notation from the original work, where one attention layer is interleaved per every $k{+}1$ total layers: h3-1 corresponds to 25\% attention, h6-1 to 14.3\%, h12-1 to 7.7\%, and h24-1 to 4\%. All configurations are available at two scales: 340M and 1.3B parameters. Within each scale, all models share identical training data, tokenizer, and hyperparameters; they differ only in architecture and attention configuration. This controlled design isolates the effect of attention from confounds such as training data or optimization differences. The zoo also includes a Transformer baseline (pure attention) at both scales.

\subsection{Task Taxonomy}\label{ssec:task_taxonomy}

We evaluate on 10 benchmarks grouped into four categories defined by the primary cognitive demand of each task. This taxonomy was fixed prior to analysis following established distinctions in the hybrid architecture literature \citep{arora2024zoology} (see Appendix~A for the confirmatory/exploratory split), not derived post hoc from observed results. The categories test whether the effect of attention on cross-architecture variance depends on task type.

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Retrieval}: LAMBADA \citep{paperno2016lambada}, which requires predicting a final word from long-range context without reasoning or world knowledge.
    \item \textbf{Comprehension}: BoolQ, a reading comprehension task with yes/no answers.
    \item \textbf{Reasoning}: HellaSwag, ARC-Challenge \citep{clark2018arc}, WinoGrande, and PIQA. COPA ($n = 100$) is supplementary; its small sample renders $\alpha$ unreliable (Appendix~\ref{app:subsample_sensitivity}).
    \item \textbf{Mixed}: ARC-Easy, MMLU, SciQ, and XStoryCloze, which combine retrieval, comprehension, and reasoning demands.
\end{itemize}

All evaluations use lm-evaluation-harness v0.4.x \citep{gao2024lmeval} with default prompts and greedy decoding; accuracy serves as the primary metric for all benchmarks. Bootstrap analyses use random seed 42 throughout.

\subsection{Convergence Metrics}\label{ssec:convergence_metrics}

We quantify cross-architecture agreement as a function of attention ratio. For each benchmark and attention ratio, we compute the cross-architecture standard deviation $\sigma$ over the three architectures' accuracy scores. To measure the relationship between attention and convergence, we compute the Spearman rank correlation $\rho$ between attention ratio rank and $\sigma$ across the five attention configurations ($n = 5$). A negative $\rho$ indicates that higher attention ratios are associated with lower cross-architecture variance.

We also report the convergence ratio $\sigma_{\text{pure}} / \sigma_{\text{h3-1}}$, defined as the standard deviation under the pure-recurrent configuration divided by that under the highest attention ratio (25\%). Values greater than 1 indicate that higher attention ratios are associated with lower cross-architecture variance.

\subsection{Per-Example Decomposition}\label{ssec:per_example_decomposition}

To move beyond aggregate metrics, we decompose performance changes at the individual-example level on four benchmarks spanning three task categories: LAMBADA (5{,}153 examples; retrieval), HellaSwag (10{,}042; reasoning), PIQA (1{,}838; reasoning, two-choice), and ARC-Easy (2{,}376; mixed). HellaSwag uses normalized accuracy (acc\_norm) as the per-example correctness criterion. Supplementary analyses on BoolQ (3{,}270; comprehension) and WinoGrande (1{,}267; reasoning, two-choice) appear in Appendix~\ref{app:boolq} and \ref{app:winogrande}. For each architecture, we compare the pure-recurrent baseline against its h3-1 hybrid (25\% attention), which maximizes the contrast between recurrent and hybrid modes; \S\ref{ssec:per_example} confirms that $\alpha$ is stable across intermediate ratios (variation $< 0.03$). We classify each example into one of three categories:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Helped}: correct in the hybrid but incorrect in the pure model.
    \item \textbf{Hurt}: correct in the pure model but incorrect in the hybrid.
    \item \textbf{Unchanged}: both models agree (both correct or both incorrect).
\end{itemize}

The net effect for each architecture is $|\text{helped}| - |\text{hurt}|$. A positive net effect means more examples transition to correct than to incorrect when attention is added; a negative net effect means the opposite.

To measure cross-architecture overlap in per-example effects, we compute the pairwise Jaccard similarity $J = |A \cap B| / |A \cup B|$ between the helped sets of two architectures and, separately, between their hurt sets. $J = 1$ indicates identical sets; $J = 0$ indicates no overlap. Low $J$ values would indicate that different architectures are affected on different subsets of examples, which would constitute per-example divergence even when aggregate performance converges. On $K$-choice tasks, binarization collapses $K{-}1$ wrong answers into a single ``incorrect'' category, potentially counting architectures as agreeing (both wrong) when they select different wrong answers. This conservative bias strengthens the multiplicity claim: true per-example divergence may exceed what binary Jaccard captures. Appendix~\ref{app:continuous_jaccard} confirms that results are robust under continuous Jaccard similarity, mitigating concerns about information loss from binarization.

\subsection{Statistical Methods}\label{ssec:statistical_methods}

All statistical tests are two-tailed. We use exact permutation tests for Spearman $\rho$ ($5! = 120$ permutations). With $n = 5$, only $|\rho| = 1.0$ achieves $p < .05$ ($p = .017$); all Spearman-based inferences are therefore descriptive and exploratory. We apply Bonferroni correction across all 10 benchmarks (adjusted threshold $\alpha / 10 = 0.005$). Bootstrap 95\% BCa confidence intervals use 10{,}000 resamples; with $n = 3$ architectures, these are heuristic indicators of effect direction. Within-architecture comparisons use Mann-Whitney U; cross-architecture comparisons use Kruskal-Wallis. Attention weight analysis extracts per-layer cosine similarity and Shannon entropy via forward hooks (Appendix~\ref{app:attention_patterns}).

